WEBVTT

00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:01.978
Surgeons cut the brain's bridge.

00:00:01.988 --> 00:00:04.316
To stop severe epilepsy, they cut the

00:00:04.326 --> 00:00:06.014
bridge between its halves.

00:00:06.024 --> 00:00:09.050
In the 1960s, Roger Sperry and Michael

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.308
Gazzaniga tested these patients.

00:00:11.318 --> 00:00:12.646
Things seen on the left go to

00:00:12.656 --> 00:00:15.146
the right half; speech mostly lives on

00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:16.022
the left.

00:00:16.032 --> 00:00:17.922
Flash a picture on the left:

00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:20.109
the patient saw nothing, he said, yet

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:21.743
his left hand drew it.

00:00:21.753 --> 00:00:23.830
Split the brain, they concluded, and you

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.428
may split the person.

00:00:25.478 --> 00:00:27.080
Sperry won a Nobel.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.467
Then, in 2017, a team ran extensive

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:33.041
tests with two split-brain patients.

00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:34.779
Both could point to things anywhere in

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:37.601
view, with either hand or aloud.

00:00:37.676 --> 00:00:40.066
Sight was split, but the team argued

00:00:40.076 --> 00:00:41.800
they were still one person.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.736
Only two patients, so
the debate continues.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:46.561
Two halves, one mind?
